WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:02.735
-Our next guest is a very funny
actor and comedian

00:00:02.735 --> 00:00:04.704
you know from his work
on "Saturday Night Live,"

00:00:04.704 --> 00:00:06.906
"Mr. Mayor," and
"The Secret Life of Pets."

00:00:06.906 --> 00:00:09.308
His children's book
"Not All Sheep Are Boring!"

00:00:09.308 --> 00:00:11.077
is available now.
Please welcome back to the show

00:00:11.077 --> 00:00:14.047
our good friend Bobby Moynihan,
everyone!

00:00:14.047 --> 00:00:15.748
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:15.748 --> 00:00:22.088 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:22.088 --> 00:00:28.460 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:33.299
Welcome to the club
of celebrity book authors.

00:00:33.299 --> 00:00:34.533
[ Laughter ]

00:00:34.533 --> 00:00:35.635
-We did it!
-We did it.

00:00:35.635 --> 00:00:38.204
-We deserve it.
-We deserve it.

00:00:38.204 --> 00:00:40.239
Now, I've written
one of these, as well, Bobby.

00:00:40.239 --> 00:00:41.740
-Yes, I read it.
-Thank you.

00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:43.443
-Thanks.
-You found the time?

00:00:43.443 --> 00:00:46.412
-I did. I did. Long one.
It was a long one.

00:00:46.412 --> 00:00:47.880
-It was a long one.

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:50.082
I actually have had parents be
like, "It was a little long."

00:00:50.082 --> 00:00:51.785
But this --
The question that I gotta ask --

00:00:51.785 --> 00:00:53.953
what was your inspiration for
"Not All Sheep Are Boring!"?

00:00:53.953 --> 00:00:56.422
And how long did it take you,
the process, to write this book?

00:00:56.422 --> 00:00:59.525
-The inspiration --
I have a daughter now.

00:00:59.525 --> 00:01:01.460
Doing a lot of bedtime reading.

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:04.397
And the topic of counting sheep
to fall asleep came up.

00:01:04.397 --> 00:01:06.499
And I thought, "Why is that?"

00:01:06.499 --> 00:01:08.768
It's 'cause they're boring.
It's 'cause they're --

00:01:08.768 --> 00:01:11.337
-Yeah.
-They're a very boring animal.

00:01:11.337 --> 00:01:13.305
And that's why people do it.

00:01:13.305 --> 00:01:15.775
So I sat in my bathroom
for an hour and I wrote a book.

00:01:15.775 --> 00:01:17.210
[ Laughter ]

00:01:19.078 --> 00:01:21.181
-So it only took an hour?

00:01:21.181 --> 00:01:24.116
-Maybe a little less
than an hour. No, no, no.

00:01:24.116 --> 00:01:26.485
No, I thought about it
for a very long time.

00:01:26.485 --> 00:01:28.288
-Oh, yeah.
You put hours and hours.

00:01:28.288 --> 00:01:30.389
-Hours of toiling
and then in the bathroom...

00:01:30.389 --> 00:01:32.791
FDADADADADA! Real quick.

00:01:32.791 --> 00:01:34.760
-Do you do your best writing
in the bathroom?

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:36.362
-I only write in the bathroom.

00:01:36.362 --> 00:01:37.931
Pandemic. I lived in
the bathroom for two years

00:01:37.931 --> 00:01:39.766
pretty much during the pandemic.

00:01:39.766 --> 00:01:41.934 line:0%
-There's a -- There's a toad
character, which is a --

00:01:41.934 --> 00:01:44.170 line:0%
Your daughter -- One of your
daughter's favorite animals.

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:45.838 line:0%
-Yeah, look at him.
He's a little toad guy.

00:01:45.838 --> 00:01:47.506 line:0%
-Yeah.
-Such a little cutie.

00:01:47.506 --> 00:01:48.842 line:0%
-How did Dorothy --

00:01:48.842 --> 00:01:50.510 line:0%
-Julie Rowan-Zoch
did the art for this.

00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:52.144 line:0%
She -- She's absolutely amazing.

00:01:52.144 --> 00:01:54.280
Dorothy --
Yeah, I like little fat toads.

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:56.315
-Uh-huh.
-I thought it was going to be

00:01:56.315 --> 00:01:58.151
a big fat toad, but he turned
out to be a little guy,

00:01:58.151 --> 00:01:59.852
and I'm fine with it.
-You know what?

00:01:59.852 --> 00:02:03.155
I heard -- I heard -- So, this
is the beautiful artwork there.

00:02:03.155 --> 00:02:05.124
-Yeah.
-But you -- you tried.

00:02:05.124 --> 00:02:07.159
You gave an early effort
to do the art yourself.

00:02:07.159 --> 00:02:09.495
-I -- Yeah.
I used to draw when I was a kid.

00:02:09.495 --> 00:02:12.164
And I was like,
"I can draw this book, too!"

00:02:12.164 --> 00:02:13.599
And I was wrong. I was wrong.

00:02:13.599 --> 00:02:15.034
-Yeah.
-I tried.

00:02:15.034 --> 00:02:16.836
-But you didn't do a terrible
job. These are way better --

00:02:16.836 --> 00:02:18.638
When I heard you were
going to do the drawings...

00:02:18.638 --> 00:02:20.172
-That's where everyone goes.

00:02:20.172 --> 00:02:21.774
Like, I remember telling them,
"I want to draw the book."

00:02:21.774 --> 00:02:23.275
And they went, "Oh, great!"

00:02:23.275 --> 00:02:24.511
-Well, this isn't bad.

00:02:24.511 --> 00:02:25.744
That's a sheep
with a secret, right?

00:02:25.744 --> 00:02:26.979
-I think he's a little --

00:02:26.979 --> 00:02:28.815
[ Laughter ]
He's evil.

00:02:28.815 --> 00:02:30.349
-Yeah, he's really evil.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:02:30.349 --> 00:02:31.751
Maybe he was
terrifying children.

00:02:31.751 --> 00:02:33.018
-But these aren't --
These are good.

00:02:33.018 --> 00:02:35.020 line:0%
-These are the picture--
[ Laughs ]

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:36.922 line:0%
Yeah, I sent them these,
and they were like,

00:02:36.922 --> 00:02:39.926 line:0%
"We're going to send you
a couple illustrators."

00:02:39.926 --> 00:02:43.162
And I'm extremely glad I did.
But I tried.

00:02:43.162 --> 00:02:45.531
-Yeah. I think you did.
Is Dorothy -- Does she like it?

00:02:45.531 --> 00:02:47.967
-Yeah, she loves it.
She loves reading in general.

00:02:47.967 --> 00:02:50.036
She likes me, which is nice.
-That's nice.

00:02:50.036 --> 00:02:52.571
-And I wrote it. Yeah, no.
She -- she really likes it.

00:02:52.571 --> 00:02:53.906
She likes "Pierre."

00:02:53.906 --> 00:02:56.876
She likes -- She likes anything,
like, spooky or weird.

00:02:56.876 --> 00:03:00.045
-Do you -- Having done a lot
of voices for animated things,

00:03:00.045 --> 00:03:02.247
does she like when
you do voices when you read?

00:03:02.247 --> 00:03:04.684
-Uh, sometimes.

00:03:04.684 --> 00:03:09.188
We do -- Like, the hour before
bed is just an improv show...

00:03:09.188 --> 00:03:10.990
-Yeah.
-...in our house.

00:03:10.990 --> 00:03:13.058
But every once in a while now,

00:03:13.058 --> 00:03:15.128
she's getting to the age
where I'm like, "Dorothy!"

00:03:15.128 --> 00:03:16.595
And she goes, "Please stop."

00:03:16.595 --> 00:03:18.665
Immediately.
Immediately, "Please stop."

00:03:18.665 --> 00:03:20.933
-[ Laughs ]
My kids hate when I do voices.

00:03:20.933 --> 00:03:22.101
-Oh, yeah.

00:03:22.101 --> 00:03:23.969
-Yeah, they just want me
to read the words.

00:03:23.969 --> 00:03:26.105
-She has a -- a Mel
from "Secret Life of Pets"

00:03:26.105 --> 00:03:27.974
that, like, when you squeeze it,
it's my voice.

00:03:27.974 --> 00:03:29.975
And she'll go -- pff --
and just smack it.

00:03:29.975 --> 00:03:32.378
Yeah. [ Laughs ]
She's not a fan of that one.

00:03:32.378 --> 00:03:33.746
-She is not --

00:03:33.746 --> 00:03:35.181
Does that mean
this improv-show thing --

00:03:35.181 --> 00:03:37.116
does that mean she does not
go to bed right away?

00:03:37.116 --> 00:03:40.920
-No. The hour
before sleep is -- is both --

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:43.189
is my wife telling both of us
to calm down.

00:03:43.189 --> 00:03:44.991
-Oh, I see. Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:03:44.991 --> 00:03:46.859
I -- I put her to bed sometimes,

00:03:46.859 --> 00:03:49.128
and she goes to bed
45 minutes later

00:03:49.128 --> 00:03:51.664
because I'm just
being a weirdo with her,

00:03:51.664 --> 00:03:53.599
and I think it's fun.
-Yeah.

00:03:53.599 --> 00:03:54.801
They're good when they're --

00:03:54.801 --> 00:03:56.402
When they're a good audience,
it's really fun.

00:03:56.402 --> 00:03:58.804
-Yeah, it is.
She's a good laugher.

00:03:58.804 --> 00:04:00.539
Yeah, she has
a lot of characters.

00:04:00.539 --> 00:04:02.709
-Oh, she does characters, too?
-Yes, very much so.

00:04:02.709 --> 00:04:04.844
Crazy shopper
is a big Dorothy character.

00:04:04.844 --> 00:04:06.779
-Really?
What does crazy shopper do?

00:04:06.779 --> 00:04:08.181
-Okay, all you have to do --

00:04:08.181 --> 00:04:09.649
If you will be me
in this situation.

00:04:09.649 --> 00:04:11.117
-Okay.

00:04:11.117 --> 00:04:13.086
-Just say, "Well,
time to open my store."

00:04:13.086 --> 00:04:14.787
-Okay.
"Well, time to open my store."

00:04:14.787 --> 00:04:16.422
-[ Screaming ]
And then she just runs in

00:04:16.422 --> 00:04:19.759
and knocks everything down.

00:04:19.759 --> 00:04:21.360
That's crazy shopper.
-Crazy shopper.

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:23.229
-I didn't -- I didn't
want to ruin your desk.

00:04:23.229 --> 00:04:24.897
-Thank you very much.
-You're welcome.

00:04:24.897 --> 00:04:26.832
-That's a good character.
-Uh, crazy shopper.

00:04:26.832 --> 00:04:27.934
Mr. Mustache.

00:04:27.934 --> 00:04:29.134
-What does Mr. Mustache do?

00:04:29.134 --> 00:04:30.503
-He's got a mustache.
-Great.

00:04:30.503 --> 00:04:32.137
-Yeah, he talks --
and he loves talking about it.

00:04:32.137 --> 00:04:33.439
-Loves talking
about the mustache.

00:04:33.439 --> 00:04:36.275
-Yeah. Baby unicorn
has to be carried everywhere.

00:04:36.275 --> 00:04:37.443
-Mm-hmm.

00:04:37.443 --> 00:04:39.511
-Baby unicorn's legs
are not very strong.

00:04:39.511 --> 00:04:41.147
-Yep.
-And then Evil Dorothy.

00:04:41.147 --> 00:04:43.716
-Evil. Wow. She has --
She's already learned

00:04:43.716 --> 00:04:45.451
that you can have
an evil version of yourself.

00:04:45.451 --> 00:04:46.919
-Yes. Evil Dorothy.
Where's my camera?

00:04:46.919 --> 00:04:48.321
This is Evil Dorothy,
is just this.

00:04:48.321 --> 00:04:50.523
She just goes...

00:04:52.625 --> 00:04:55.394
[ Laughter ]

00:04:55.394 --> 00:04:57.496
-And we should note --
she's 19, right?

00:04:57.496 --> 00:04:58.731
-She's 19 years old, yes.

00:04:58.731 --> 00:05:01.000
[ Laughter ]

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:02.301
-Hey --
-She's a writer on your show!

00:05:02.301 --> 00:05:03.936
-Yeah, she's a writer.
She's great. We love you.

00:05:03.936 --> 00:05:06.705
Well, we love Dorothy.
We don't love Evil Dorothy.

00:05:06.705 --> 00:05:08.407
Speaking of characters, you did

00:05:08.407 --> 00:05:10.710
one of my all-time favorites,
Anthony Crispino, right here.

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:13.011
-Oh, look at these children.
-Yeah, look at these children.

00:05:13.011 --> 00:05:15.315
-Wow!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:17.049 --> 00:05:18.750
-I feel like even --

00:05:18.750 --> 00:05:21.721
even the colors of our clothes
have gone gray.

00:05:21.721 --> 00:05:23.656
-[ Laughs ]
Yeah, life has just gotten --

00:05:23.656 --> 00:05:25.624
-Yeah, everything
is a little more muted.

00:05:25.624 --> 00:05:27.160
-Life has just gotten gloomier.

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:28.827
Look at that background.
-Alright.

00:05:28.827 --> 00:05:31.730
So, Secondhand News,
Anthony Crispino --

00:05:31.730 --> 00:05:33.265
he was famously -- I mean,

00:05:33.265 --> 00:05:34.867
it was a character
you and Colin Jost wrote.

00:05:34.867 --> 00:05:37.069
-Yeah, yeah.
-But it was about a guy who

00:05:37.069 --> 00:05:38.737
sort of got the news secondhand

00:05:38.737 --> 00:05:40.205
and maybe got
some of the details wrong.

00:05:40.205 --> 00:05:42.341
-Yeah, was always afraid
of people hearing it, too.

00:05:42.341 --> 00:05:44.410
-Yeah, he had
top-secret information.

00:05:44.410 --> 00:05:46.178
He was fine to say it
on "Weekend Update,"

00:05:46.178 --> 00:05:47.412
which is national television.

00:05:47.412 --> 00:05:48.981
-Fine to say it
on national television, yeah.

00:05:48.981 --> 00:05:50.449
-Could we do a few?
Could I feed you a few

00:05:50.449 --> 00:05:52.151
and see what you think
Crispino might say about it?

00:05:52.151 --> 00:05:53.385
-Sure. Let's do it.
It's been a while.

00:05:53.385 --> 00:05:54.620
-Okay. So here we go.

00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:57.222
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:57.222 --> 00:06:01.293
So, you know, I think the way
we would do this -- Yeah, yeah.

00:06:01.293 --> 00:06:03.028
Hey, Anthony, I don't know

00:06:03.028 --> 00:06:04.997
if you heard about these
Mar-a-lago documents. They...

00:06:04.997 --> 00:06:07.133
-Oh, yeah. You hear
about this thing, though?

00:06:08.801 --> 00:06:11.103
Oh, it's so good to see you.
-It is good.

00:06:11.103 --> 00:06:13.005
I really like that we're --
This is a real flashback.

00:06:13.005 --> 00:06:14.873
-It really feels
very calm, very normal.

00:06:14.873 --> 00:06:16.508
You hear about this thing,
though?

00:06:16.508 --> 00:06:17.643
-What's that, Anthony?

00:06:17.643 --> 00:06:21.180
-Marge Simpson
found some documents.

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:22.948
Turns out she's dating
Master Splinter

00:06:22.948 --> 00:06:24.917
from the Ninja Turtles.

00:06:24.917 --> 00:06:27.653
Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles.
You heard it here first.

00:06:27.653 --> 00:06:29.689
-It's not -- it's not Marge
Simpson's. It's Mar-a-lago.

00:06:29.689 --> 00:06:33.325
-Eh...pretty sure
it's Marge Simpson, though.

00:06:33.325 --> 00:06:35.661
Pretty sure it's Marge Simpson
dating Splinter

00:06:35.661 --> 00:06:36.862
from the Ninja Turtles.

00:06:36.862 --> 00:06:40.199
-No. It's "special master."
-Highly topical!

00:06:40.199 --> 00:06:43.536
-It was --
It was rumored...

00:06:43.536 --> 00:06:45.504
[ Laughter ]

00:06:45.504 --> 00:06:47.739
It was rumored Harry Styles
spit on Chris Pine.

00:06:47.739 --> 00:06:50.943
-Ohh, boy.

00:06:50.943 --> 00:06:52.111
You hear about this?

00:06:52.111 --> 00:06:53.880
Even more topical,
Harry Hamlin...

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:55.080
[ Laughter ]

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:56.649
-Harry Hamlin?
-Yeah, Harry Hamlin.

00:06:56.649 --> 00:06:58.250
You know the guy
with the nice hair?

00:06:58.250 --> 00:06:59.351
-Yeah, from "L.A. Law."

00:06:59.351 --> 00:07:00.452
-Yeah.
Of course, from "L.A. Law."

00:07:00.452 --> 00:07:02.555
-Yeah.
-Where you think he's from?

00:07:02.555 --> 00:07:05.223
"St. Elsewhere"?

00:07:05.223 --> 00:07:08.494
He split a tree in half
with his head. Pine tree.

00:07:08.494 --> 00:07:10.696
-No.
Harry Styles spit on Chris Pine.

00:07:10.696 --> 00:07:14.033
-Eh, pretty sure it was
the other way around, Seth!

00:07:14.033 --> 00:07:16.735
I can't even get
that high anymore. I'm so old.

00:07:16.735 --> 00:07:18.437
-Yeah, that part
of your voice is gone.

00:07:18.437 --> 00:07:20.439
Yeah, thank you for stopping by,
Anthony Crispino.

00:07:20.439 --> 00:07:22.274
It was great to see you.
-Thank you for having me.

00:07:22.274 --> 00:07:25.545
[ Cheers and applause ]
-You're also...

00:07:25.545 --> 00:07:27.513
-That was -- That was
truly fun and cathartic.

00:07:27.513 --> 00:07:29.281
Thank you for that.
-It was great to do.

00:07:29.281 --> 00:07:30.582
I mean, we -- it was a --

00:07:30.582 --> 00:07:32.150
it was always a very fun time
with you out there.

00:07:32.150 --> 00:07:33.552
You were -- you were --
-The best. That was my --

00:07:33.552 --> 00:07:36.121
My most favorite moments
were with you on "Update."

00:07:36.121 --> 00:07:38.824
-Yeah, Anthony Crispino
would get very high in a way

00:07:38.824 --> 00:07:41.260
that I feel like only dogs
could hear him at some point.

00:07:41.260 --> 00:07:42.728
-Yeah, that was the point.

00:07:42.728 --> 00:07:44.363
I believe we did
that joke once where --

00:07:44.363 --> 00:07:45.665
I think you're right.
Dogs started barking. Yep.

00:07:45.665 --> 00:07:47.266
-Where dogs started barking.

00:07:47.266 --> 00:07:48.968
-You're doing the
"Batman: Audio Adventures."

00:07:48.968 --> 00:07:50.402
You're doing another season
of that.

00:07:50.402 --> 00:07:52.204
That's our friend, Dennis
McNicholas, who wrote those.

00:07:52.204 --> 00:07:54.906
-Yeah, the best. He let me
play the Penguin, my dream.

00:07:54.906 --> 00:07:56.341
-Yeah.
-It's so much fun.

00:07:56.341 --> 00:07:58.344
-And then you're also --
You just finished

00:07:58.344 --> 00:08:00.579
wrapping a film where
Jerry Seinfeld directed.

00:08:00.579 --> 00:08:03.916
-Yeah, yeah. It's called
"Unfrosted" for Netflix.

00:08:03.916 --> 00:08:06.418
All about the story of
how the Pop-Tart was made.

00:08:06.418 --> 00:08:09.689
Extremely true story.

00:08:09.689 --> 00:08:11.057
[ Laughter ]

00:08:11.057 --> 00:08:12.892
-Like documentary quality.
-Documentary --

00:08:12.892 --> 00:08:14.960
Getting to the hard stuff
about the Pop-Tart.

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:16.628
Yeah, yeah.
-That's very exciting.

00:08:16.628 --> 00:08:20.066
I mean, I find it to be a trip
just to talk to Jerry Seinfeld.

00:08:20.066 --> 00:08:22.935
It must be amazing to be
directed by Jerry Seinfeld.

00:08:22.935 --> 00:08:24.436
-It is. It's very strange.

00:08:24.436 --> 00:08:27.039
Just hearing his voice
in general go like --

00:08:27.039 --> 00:08:28.607
It's Jerry Seinfeld.
-Yeah.

00:08:28.607 --> 00:08:31.243
-And you feel like
someone's doing a bit.

00:08:31.243 --> 00:08:32.645
I feel like
Jay Pharoah is around.

00:08:32.645 --> 00:08:34.213
-Yeah.
-Because -- Because it's just

00:08:34.213 --> 00:08:36.215
somebody doing his voice,
but then he's looking at you

00:08:36.215 --> 00:08:37.983
talking and saying your name,
and you're going,

00:08:37.983 --> 00:08:39.651
"Okay, Jerry Seinfeld."

00:08:39.651 --> 00:08:42.188
Like, it's hard
to get past that.

00:08:42.188 --> 00:08:44.557
-Yeah, well, I'm very excited
to see this,

00:08:44.557 --> 00:08:46.759
and I'm always excited
to have you here.

00:08:46.759 --> 00:08:49.695
Congratulations one more time
on the book, everybody.

00:08:49.695 --> 00:08:53.565
It's called "I'm Not Scared,
You're Scared!"

00:08:53.565 --> 00:08:56.435
-Wait a minute!
-Alright. Hold on.

00:08:56.435 --> 00:08:59.438
"Not All Sheep Are Boring!"
I'm sorry. It's on sale now.

00:08:59.438 --> 00:09:01.540
We'll be back
with more "Late Night."

00:09:01.540 --> 00:09:03.009
-Thank you, buddy.

